.TH DOSSRV 4
.SH NAME
dossrv, 9660srv \- DOS/Windows and ISO 9660 file systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dossrv
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-F ]
.RB [ \-c ]
[
.BI -f " devicefile"
]
[
.BI -m " mountpoint"
]
[
.BI -S " sectors/track"
]
[
.BI -N " buffers"
]
.PP
.B 9660srv
.RB [ \-rab ]
.I cdfile
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dossrv
makes the contents of a DOS/Windows file system visible in the Inferno name space.
The options are:
.TP
.B -v
print a debugging trace, including 9P messages, on standard error
.TP
.B -F
display FAT information
.TP
.B -c
display cluster data and I/O activity
.TP
.B -s
standard ouput is connected to a client; serve that
.TP
.BI -f " devicefile"
the DOS/Windows file system image; defaults to
.BR /dev/hd0disk .
.TP
.BI -m " mountpoint"
The directory where the contents of the file system should appear; defaults to
.BR /n/dos .
.IP
This is the ONLY way to say where to mount.
.I Dossrv
takes no positional arguments \(em unlike
.I 9660srv
beside it, which takes two \(em and used to ignore one silently and mount
on the default instead, which on a board where
.B /n/dos
is already a writable mount looks exactly like success.  It now refuses a
stray argument.
.TP
.BI -S  " sectors/track"
The number of sectors per track. The default is 9.
A track is the unit of device I/O, so this sets how much is read or
written at a time and, with
.BR -N ,
how much is cached.
.TP
.BI -N " buffers"
How many tracks are cached.  The default is 20.
.TP
.B -A
Allocate a cluster without first checking that it reads.
.IP
By default
.I dossrv
reads every cluster off the device before allocating it, and marks one
that will not read bad in the FAT so it is never handed out again.  That
exists because of a real failing card: deleting a file and copying it
again put it back on the same dead page and it failed at the same byte
every time, because a free cluster is a free cluster and nothing ever
asked whether it worked.
.IP
The cost is one device read per cluster allocated, which is
.I one cluster read for every cluster written.
Measured on
.IR sdmem (3)
over a 2.6MB write: 22 per cent of the elapsed time with a free medium
and 39 per cent with 500us charged per transfer, and the read count
scales as 1/clustersize \(em 1284 reads at 2KB clusters against 165 at
16KB.
.B -A
turns it off, which is how that was measured, and
.B /tests/dossrv-badclust
is what checks the behaviour it turns off.
.TP
.B -B
Scan every cluster for readability and report what cannot be read,
without changing anything.
.TP
.B -b
The same scan, marking what cannot be read bad in the FAT.
.PP
.BR -S " and " -N
interact and the second exists so that a measurement of the first can be
controlled: twenty 32-sector tracks is 320KB of cache where twenty
9-sector tracks is 90KB, so varying the track size alone varies the
cache size with it.  Over
.I fatstress
on a FAT32 image with 512-byte clusters, quadrupling the cache at 9
sectors moves the device read count by 1.6 per cent, while 16-sector
tracks nearly halve it and 32-sector tracks cut it to a third; at a FIXED
cache budget, 32-sector tracks thrash.  Larger tracks move more bytes for
fewer operations, and which way that trades depends on the medium.
.PP
At EOF
.I dossrv
prints one line to standard error giving the track size, the cache size,
the number of track reads and writes it asked the device for, and how
many of the reads were read-modify-write \(em a track fetched only so
that the sectors the client did not write would survive being written
back with the ones it did.  That last number is the cost of the track
being larger than the write, and on
.I fatstress
it is three to six per cent of the reads.
.PP
A further line per region of the medium \(em boot/reserved, FAT, root
directory, data \(em says where the reads went and how many of them found
sectors of that track already in the cache.  The totals alone cannot
distinguish a FAT being re-read from a sweep of the data region, and
.B /doc/open-issues-plan.ms
records two sessions attributing one to the other: a 2.6MB write was
reported as "one FAT lookup per cluster allocated" when the FAT accounted
for two reads of the six, and the thousand-odd others were the
per-cluster verification read described under
.B -A
below.
.PP
A caution about both lines: they count what
.I dossrv
asked the DEVICE for, which is not what the device did.  The
per-allocation verification read goes straight to the device rather than
through this cache and appears in NEITHER line; on a kernel with
.IR sdmem (3)
the driver's own counters see it.  Two instruments disagreeing by a
factor of two hundred is how it was found.
.PP
.I 9660srv
performs a similar function for files (notably CDROMs) in ISO9660 format,
including the Rock Ridge extension and its Plan 9 variant.
It makes the contents of
.I cdfile
visible in the name space at
.IR dir .
The options are those of
.IR bind (1):
.BR \-r ,
to replace the contents of
.IR dir ;
.BR \-a ,
to make a union directory with the CD contents after the contents of
.IR dir ;
.BR \-b ,
to make a union directory with the CD contents before the contents of
.IR dir .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/dossrv.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/9660srv.b
.SH TESTING
.IR Fatstress (1)
and
.B /tests/dossrv-stress.sh
write, rename and remove files through
.I dossrv
on three file systems \- FAT16, FAT32 with 512-byte clusters and
files above cluster 65535, and FAT32 with 4KB clusters \- and then
run
.B "fsck_msdos -n"
over each image.
All three pass, image clean.
The Raspberry Pi 3B kernel runs a smaller version of the same checks
against its own card at boot, in
.BR /os/inferno/init/raspi3binit.b ,
and prints FAT-RENAME-OK, FAT-CLOBBER-OK and FAT-BIG-OK.
.SH BUGS
.I Dossrv
cannot handle all the FAT32 extensions.
.PP
These were all found by a Raspberry Pi 3B damaging the card it boots
from, one boot at a time, and are fixed:
a rename kept only the low sixteen bits of a start cluster, so a file
above cluster 65535 was rewritten to point somewhere else;
a FAT32 root directory could not grow past its first cluster;
a rename onto a name that already existed removed the source entry
before finding out it could not finish, leaving neither file and
reporting a phase error;
and
.I doremove
followed a long name into the sector before it even when the chain
had already ended, deleting the long-name entries of a different
file, which left that file with a live directory entry and no name.
